#{[17592186595959 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫6x$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{6x}{{e}^{x}}+3)=0+3=3.\n$$"] [17592186595964 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186595964 :question/worked-solution "The sine function is a bounded, oscillating function with\n\n$$\n|\\operatorname{sin}⁡x|\\le 1.\n$$\n\nSo the numerator here is a bounded, oscillating function with \n\n$$\n1\\le 2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x\\le 3.\n$$\n\nHowever, the denominator ${x}^{2}+x$ grows without bound as $x\\to \\infty .$ Since the denominator grows much faster than the (bounded) numerator, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x}=0.\n$$\n\nWe can see this from the graph of $y=\\frac{2+\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{{x}^{2}+x},$ shown below:\n\n![](images/0e/0eb0389561fc6c754431fcf7fcb63f5b03ad80f4de6a34ab407dbbdc079e77c4.png)"] [17592186595973 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x){e}^{-3x}+2).$"] [17592186595980 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{e}^{3x}}+1)=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186596004 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=\n$$"] [17592186596004 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-3)^{2}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}=\\infty\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{(x-3)^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x-1)}+10)=\\infty\n$$"] [17592186596005 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\,\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186596701 :question/problem "The function $g$ is defined as $$\n\\displaystyle g(x)={\\int }_{-5}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t.\n$$\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to -5}\\,\\frac{g(x)}{{x}^{2}+12x+35}=$\n{{field-1}}."] [17592186599786 :question/problem "![](images/21/21d8823484fa5c17c1a0ea33a74a733116816100e169ca675c7f1216730e4328.png)\n\nFind $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)$ for the function above."] [17592186599786 :question/worked-solution "As $x$ approaches $1,$ the value of $f(x)$ approaches $3.$ Consequently, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}f(x)=3\\,.\n$$\n\nRemember, it does not matter that the actual value of $f(1)$ is $2.$ All that matters is that as $x$ approaches $1,$ the function $f(x)$ *looks like* it is getting closer and closer to $3.$"] [17592186601505 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186601505 :question/worked-solution "Let's sketch the graph of $y={log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right).$\n\n![](images/9f/9f9d26034e3aa5010f6be4045393874de0268ac50226675ad8233dedb1cabefd.png)\n\nThe function is not defined for $x\\le 0.$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{-}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$\n\nHowever, the function is defined for $x>0.$ Moreover,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {2}^{+}}{log}_{6}⁡\\left(\\frac{x-2}{7}\\right)=-\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186601940 :question/problem "Find the function $f(x)$ and the value of $b,$ given that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\n$\\,f(x)=$\n{{field-1}} $\\,$ and \n$\\,b=$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186601940 :question/worked-solution "A definite integral can be defined as the limit of a left Riemann sum as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{a}^{b}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}f({x}_{k})\\cdot Δx\n$$\n\nwhere $Δx=\\frac{b-a}{n}$ and ${x}_{k}=a+kΔx.$\n\nNotice that we're given that the lower limit $a=1.$ \n\nComparing the given summation with the general expression above, we see that $Δx=\\frac{1}{n}.$ Therefore, \n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{n}=\\frac{b-1}{n}\\,⟹\\,b=2.\n$$\n\nConsequently, ${x}_{k}=1+\\frac{k}{n}$ and $f({x}_{k})=4{x}_{k}^{4}.$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{1}^{2}4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}[4{(1+\\frac{k}{n})}^{4}\\cdot \\frac{1}{n}].\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \n\n$$\nf(x)=4{x}^{4}\\,\\text{and}\\,b=2.\n$$"] [17592186602406 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\,(-1-x-{x}^{2})=${{field-1}}"] [17592186602410 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{62+f(x)}.$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{4}^{+}}\\frac{\\sqrt{x}-2}{62+f(x)}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186602422 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x).$ If the limit does not exist, write \"DNE\" (without the quotation marks).\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,\\infty }f(x)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186602430 :question/problem "${f}^{′}(x)=\\lim_{h\\to 0}(-3{x}^{2}-3xh-{h}^{2})=${{field-1}}"] [17592186602437 :question/problem "The instantaneous rate of change of $f(x)$ at $x=1$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle {f}^{′}(1)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,1}\\frac{f(x)-f(1)}{x-1}=\\lim_{x\\to 1}(-1\\,-\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{x}^{2}).$"] [17592186602457 :question/problem "The derivative of $f(x)$ is defined by\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\frac{\\text{d}f}{\\text{d}x}=\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}\\frac{f(x+h)-f(x)}{h}=\\lim_{h\\to \\,0}(-3{x}^{2}-\n$$\n{{field-1}} \n$-\\,{h}^{2}).$"] [17592186603006 :question/problem "Write down an expression for the area $A$ as a definite integral, and express the integral as the limit of a left Riemann sum.\n\n$$\n\\displaystyle A={\\int }_{2}^{5}\n$$\n{{field-1}}\n$$\n\\displaystyle \\text{d}x=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{\\sum }_{k=0}^{n-1}\n$$\n{{field-2}}"] [17592186603039 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{f(x)}{{x}^{2}-1}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186604024 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186604024 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${x}^{4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2x)}{{x}^{4}}=0.\n$$"] [17592186604025 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}.$"] [17592186604025 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5x)}{{e}^{x}}=0.\n$$"] [17592186604060 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{5}{{x}^{4}}=$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186604080 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6x)\\,{e}^{-4x}-1)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186605097 :question/problem "Evaluate the limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\frac{|10x+7|}{2x+1}=\n$$\n\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186605110 :question/problem "![](images/70/701cd49e5926f07a4c3a9a145660aaa205119da1f4ccf8b174706fc8d70dec24.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true with respect to the function $y=f(x)$ whose graph is plotted above. \n\nI. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{-}}f(x)$ is finite\n\nII. $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{+}}f(x)$ is **not** finite\n\nIII. $f(x)$ has a discontinuity at $x=-6$"] [17592186605110 :question/worked-solution "By inspecting the graph, we can conclude the following:\n\n- Statement I is false. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{-}}f(x)=-\\infty ,$ which is not finite.\n\n- Statement II is true. We get $\\lim_{x\\to \\,-{6}^{+}}f(x)=\\infty .$\n\n- Statement III is true. The graph of the function has a vertical asymptote exactly at $x=-6$ and it's impossible to draw its graph without taking our pencil off of the paper."] [17592186605113 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }(9{x}^{9})=${{field-1}}"] [17592186605134 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{u\\to -\\infty }{(3{u}^{3}-u)}^{5}.$"] [17592186605142 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(6{x}^{9})=\n$$"] [17592186605147 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{z\\to -\\infty }{(4{z}^{3}-3)}^{3}.$"] [17592186605854 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{{e}^{n/3}}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{log}_{2}⁡n}{{e}^{n/3}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges, and its limit is $0.$"] [17592186605861 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}-3)=-3+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}=0.\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n-3+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(14n)}{n}=-3+0=-3.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence converges and its limit is $-3.$"] [17592186605886 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{4{n}^{2}}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{4{n}^{2}}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [17592186610950 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 1}\\sqrt[3]{2-3x}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186611600 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{6x}{\\sqrt{9x+1}-\\sqrt{6x+1}}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186611648 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\,1/2}(\\frac{\\pi }{3}+2\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(x)).$"] [17592186613214 :question/problem "Find $\\lim_{x\\to (-1,-1)}x\\sqrt{{y}^{2}+1}.$"] [17592186615373 :question/problem "$\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\frac{y(x)-1}{\\operatorname{tan}⁡x}=${{field-1}}"] [17592186616147 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\n$$"] [17592186616147 :question/worked-solution "Let's recall the graph of $y=\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$\n\n![](images/44/44ba48c2eebd00aac41e09413f328906eea3148be2fcec2ed4700852d7bd4802.png)\n\nAs $x$ decreases, the values of $y$ range between $-\\infty$ and $\\infty$ periodically with period $\\pi .$ Therefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\operatorname{tan}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2})=\\text{DNE.}\n$$"] [17592186619722 :question/worked-solution "To determine whether $f(x)$ is left-continuous at $x=c,$ we need to check whether the left-sided limit is equal to the function value.\n\nLet's check this for $x=-3$, at $x=1$ and at  $x=4.$\n\n- At $x=-3$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,(-3)^{-}}f(x)=-2=f(-3).\n$$\n\n- At $x=1$ the function is left-continuous because\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\,{1}^{-}}f(x)=-2=f(1).\n$$\n\n- At $x=4$ the function is not left-continuous because it's undefined at this point.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is $x=-3$ and $x=1$ only."] [17592186619758 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=3$ and $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}f(x)=-2,$ then what is $\\lim_{x\\to {3}^{+}}(f(x)-f(3))?$"] [17592186619781 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to \\pi /4}\\operatorname{cot}⁡\\left(\\frac{2x}{3}\\right).$"] [17592186619786 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{cot}⁡x.$"] [17592186619814 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to 0}2\\operatorname{csc}⁡(x+\\frac{\\pi }{2}).$"] [17592186620817 :question/problem "Calculate $\\lim_{x\\to -\\infty }\\,\\,\\frac{{x}^{5}+4}{3x-6}.$"] [17592186629292 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}\\,\\frac{5\\operatorname{sin}⁡x}{6x}=\n$$"] [17592186629313 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nTo find the limit, we calculate\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{log}_{4}⁡n}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{n}}{{log}_{4}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges."] [17592186630260 :question/problem "If $f(x)$ is right-continuous at $x=-1$ and $f(-1)=16,$ then what is \n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}\\sqrt{4f(x)}?\n$$"] [17592186634587 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{-2n/3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186634644 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{x}{n})}^{-\\pi n/2}=\n$$"] [17592186634665 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{4n}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186638264 :question/problem "Evaluate $\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3).$"] [17592186638264 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since ${e}^{3x}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x$ for large values of $x,$ the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{{e}^{3x}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡2x}+3)=\\infty .\n$$"] [17592186638638 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\frac{1}{8}"] [17592186638678 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=3"] [17592186638695 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-1"] [17592186638697 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186638763 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\frac{2}{3}"] [17592186638829 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=2"] [17592186638853 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186638946 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=0"] [17592186638976 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=0"] [17592186638993 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=-2"] [17592186639256 :question/worked-solution "To compute $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)}f(x),$ we must compute the left and right-sided limits separately.\n\nHowever, the left-sided limit $\\lim_{x\\to \\,(1/2)^{-}}f(x)$ does not exist since $f(x)$ is not defined on the interval $x\\in (-\\frac{1}{2},\\frac{1}{2}).$ \n\nConsequently, the overall limit does not exist either:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to (1/2)}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186639267 :question/worked-solution "Approaching from the left, we have $x<0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=x.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}x=0\\,.\n$$\n\nApproaching from the right, we have $x>0,$ so we use the expression $f(x)=3.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}3=3\\,.\n$$\n\nSince the two limits are different, we can conclude that \n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 0}f(x)=\\text{DNE}.\n$$"] [17592186647763 :question/problem "$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}-2)=\n$$"] [17592186647763 :question/worked-solution "Both the numerator and denominator approach $\\infty$ as $x\\to \\infty .$ However, since $(x-1)^{4}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)$ for large values of $x,$ the denominator is growing much faster than the numerator. Consequently, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}=0.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to \\infty }(\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7x)}{(x-1)^{4}}-2)=0-2=-2\n$$"] [17592186650331 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{+}}\\left(\\frac{5}{3{x}^{4}}\\right)=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186654751 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{n{x}^{3}/3}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186654792 :question/problem "Calculate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{(1+\\frac{1}{n})}^{nx/y}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186655762 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\text{DNE},\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=3"] [17592186655855 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=1"] [17592186655943 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=3"] [17592186656013 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=\\text{DNE}"] [17592186656029 :answer/value "\\lim_{x\\to {0}^{-}}f(x)=0,\\,\\lim_{x\\to \\,(\\pi /2)^{+}}f(x)=1"] [17592186660350 :question/problem "Evaluate the following limit:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{x\\to 36}\\frac{x-36}{\\sqrt{x}-6}=\n$$\n{{field-1}}"] [17592186664092 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}+2)=2+\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nHence, \n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }(\\frac{4{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡n}+2)=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges"] [17592186667622 :question/worked-solution "Recall that for large values of $n,$ we have\n\n$$\nn!≫{e}^{n}≫{n}^{m}≫\\operatorname{ln}⁡(n),\n$$\n\nwhere $≫$ means \"much greater than,\" and $m>0.$\n\nWe calculate the limit of the sequence as $n$ approaches infinity:\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }{a}_{n}=\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}.\n$$\n\nBoth the numerator and denominator approach infinity as $n\\to \\infty .$ However, the numerator is growing much faster than the denominator, and we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\lim_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{{n}^{2}}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(6n)}=\\infty .\n$$\n\nTherefore, the sequence diverges"]}
